{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1772644249,"nonce":"YUKMYFrXmPISaL24NwvhxhJ/3DM=","title":"Long-term: replace hardcoded intervention types with flexible UI-driven state targeting","message":"Currently, intervention components like `NetworkConstantInput` and `ConstantInput` are separate Python classes with hardcoded parameter schemas (`unit_spec` with NaN masking vs `arrays`). This distinction encodes a domain-specific pattern (target specific network units vs offset whole signals) into hard Python types.\n\n## The problem\nAs the Studio matures, users should be able to select any part of the model state through the UI and define how to intervene on it (constant offset, clamp, scale, noise, etc.) without needing pre-baked component types per intervention shape. The current approach requires a new Python class for each combination of (target selector × intervention type).\n\n## Preliminary design direction\nAn intervention node in the graph should have:\n1. A **target selector**: UI-driven — user picks a state path (e.g., \"hidden state of layer 2, units 10–30\"), analogous to Penzai selectors\n2. An **intervention type**: constant, noise, clamp, etc. — chosen from a small fixed set\n3. **Parameters**: determined by the intervention type, not the target shape\n\nThis would make `NetworkConstantInput`, `ConstantInput`, and any future variants derivable from a single generic `Intervention` component type. `NetworkConstantInput` exists specifically because the unit_spec NaN masking pattern cannot be expressed with the plain `ConstantInput` schema — in a flexible system this would just be the target selector doing the masking.\n\n## Related\n- Penzai selector API (issue 7c0c4b2) — the selector mechanism is the same underlying need\n- Typed subgraph taxonomy (issue 6116155) — state targeting requires knowing the structure of what you are targeting","files":null}]}